      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You can think of Bernoulli's principle as the \"pull\" creating a vacuum effect on top, where the faster moving, lower-pressure air seems to draw the wing upwards. Newton's third law is the \"push,\" representing the wing physically deflecting the mass of air downwards, which in turn pushes the wing up. Imagine the top surface as a low-pressure system delicately \"pulling\" the wing, while the bottom and angle of attack are actively \"pushing\" against the air to generate upward thrust.",
